### Documentation Rules

> Rule of thumb: stable material that a second task will reuse goes to `.docs/`; anything that only serves one work episode goes to `.project/`.

- **`.docs/` shared material**: environment and access details, feature lists, API/data-reporting specs, resource and component inventories, acceptance and test reports — relatively stable material reused across tasks
- **`.project/` workspace**: requirement discussions, design notes, demo and verification runs, debugging, Q&A records, development plans and retrospectives
- `.docs/` groups material into category directories with a two-digit numeric prefix for ordering: `01-environments/` (fixed), `02-feature-list/`, `03-data-reporting/`; `00-` can be reserved for overview or glossary material
- **Do not use `YYYYMMDD-` date directories inside `.docs/`**; append new categories at the end of the numbering — never renumber or reuse an old number
- `.docs/README.md` is the shared-material index: register every new, moved, or split category there
- Keep each shared document and its attachments in exactly one place; topic directories under `.project/` link to `.docs/` with relative paths instead of copying files
- When several versions of a document exist, keep only the latest and record the version in the filename or the body — not in a dated directory
- **One topic, one directory** under `.project/`: tasks on the same topic share a single `<topic>/` directory. For complex work, use numbered subdirectories such as `01-discussion` inside that directory instead of creating another topic directory
- **One fact, one place**: environments, versions, quotas, conclusions, and task status appear only in the file that defines them; everything else links to them. Rewrite the original sentence when content changes — never append "update" or "follow-up" sections; leave history to git
- **Status as front matter**: every body document under `.project/` / `.docs/` starts with YAML front matter containing `status` (`active` / `reference` / `superseded` / `evidence` / `archived`), `owner_task`, and `updated_at`; retired documents add `superseded_by`. See the [Task Register](.project/INDEX.md)
- {{OTHER_DOCUMENTATION_REQUIREMENTS}}

### Environment Rules

- Environment and access details are shared material: keep them in `.docs/01-environments/`, one file per environment named `test.md` / `uat.md` / `prd.md`
- Only create files for environments that actually exist: do not invent files or placeholder content for missing environments — note them in the "Environment Index" instead
- Edit the environment file in place when details change; do not keep historical versions. Mask sensitive fields per team policy

### Task Tracking

> One state source, one detail location: `.project/INDEX.md` is the single source of truth and carries the Assignee column teams reconcile against; `.project/` holds bodies and evidence, `.docs/` holds reusable material. The round's scope comes from the session instruction and is never written into a shared file.

- **Entry protocol**: read `.project/INDEX.md` first, run `python3 scripts/check-project.py`, and treat only documents with `status: active` as execution input
- **The round's scope comes from the session instruction**: with an explicit ID list, touch only those rows; without one, take rows that are `active` and assigned to you, and state which rows you are about to touch before editing
- Never start a task from "pending confirmation / not implemented / deferred" found in `superseded`, `archived`, or `evidence` documents; agent session memory and logs are append-only records, not a status source
- Create `.project/YYYYMMDD-<topic>/` for a new task and assign it a stable ID in `INDEX.md` with an assignee, closing condition, and evidence path; merge follow-up work on the same topic into the existing directory
- Break large stages into a subtask chain in `INDEX.md` (`S1` -> `S1.1`); each item states action, artifact, and completion condition. Express the current stage as a group heading and put blockers and next actions in the Notes column of `blocked` rows
- One row, one owner: Assignee holds a single name; on handover change it and write in Notes "how far it got and how to restart", without opening a new task
- Change status by rewriting the original `INDEX.md` row and its `updated_at`, plus the front matter of the linked body document; when removing a conclusion, remove its links and references in the same change
- Mark a task `done` only when evidence exists on disk and the closing condition is met; deferred, blocked, and disabled are not done. Commit, push, merge, and deploy are separately authorized steps
- Promote lasting conclusions into `.docs/`; do not pile process detail into topic directories
- The playbook lives in [workflow](.project/rules/workflow.md) (new task / stopping halfway / interruption / resuming)

